dules are also a way to provide  opinionated infrastructure using  terraform's ability to provide fixed  values to implement your organization's  best practices like for example you  could ensure that GK clusters have  Network policy enabled while authoring  your own modules can help you codify  your organization's best practices for  security or performance you can also  find modules provided by Google for your  use or reference in the description  below alternatively if you are deeply  invested in tooling from the kubernetes  ecosystem you can also evaluate defining  your gke clusters using something called  config connector  using kubernetes custom resources and  controllers built by Google you can use  config connector to write your Cloud  infrastructure including your gke  clusters using the kubernetes API this  allows you to use tooling by customize  and kept to templateize and package your  manifest defining your infrastructure in  the same way you might for workload  definitions in kubernetes finally  whereas you may run the terraform CLI  packaged in steps of a pipeline or some  automation tool config connector is run  via kubernetes controller this means  that they are constantly driving  resource current state to your desired  state  this adds in an element of diff  reconciliation which can help prevent  drift or unwanted changes made manually  to your set of gke clusters  and if you want those controllers for  config connector managed for you by  Google you can check out a service  called config controller alright so if  we use either of the aforementioned  tools right we should have our GK  clusters provision  now what now we might want to provision  common kubernetes resources to these  clusters that teams create  Google's config sync helps you achieve  this via pattern called git Ops in short  meaning that we are using git as a  source of truth from which multiple  clusters can pull kubernetes manifests  with config sync each cluster will have  a local component that pulls manifests  from a git repository and applies them  as kubernetes resources to their  respective cluster  like config connector config sync  performs diff reconciliation but for  these kubernetes resources that belong  in each gke cluster  this means that each cluster side  component of config sync is constantly  ensuring that resources in the git repo  match what exists in the cluster so if  you want consistent cluster roles  storage classes demon sets or network  policies you can use config sync to make  sure they'll always be present in your  gke clusters now there's a lot in this  space but hopefully this gives you the  lay of the land and a place to start  there are a few next steps if you're  just getting started on this journey  that I might recommend for example you  can evaluate how you can enforce the  cluster configurations and kubernetes  resources adhere to policies that you  define using projects like open policy  agent  you can also examine if you could  benefit from more granular control with  config sync by having subsets of  clusters pull subsets of resources that  match their clusters profile but perhaps  most important is to now look at these  tools and principles in the context of  your team your people and your  organization  so that it makes sense with what works  for y'all check out the links in the  description below to learn more and as  always thank you for your time  [Music]  thank you  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Laughter]  [Music]
 

